__d(function(e,a,t,o,i,n,s){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:3,chapter_title:"Data Handling",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the arithmetic mean? How is it calculated?",back:"The arithmetic mean (or average) is the sum of all observations divided by the number of observations. Formula: Mean = (Sum of all observations) / (Number of observations). Example: Data = 8, 12, 6, 10, 14. Sum = 50. n = 5. Mean = 50/5 = 10. The mean represents the 'fair share' \u2014 what each value would be if all were made equal.",memory_hook:"Mean = TOTAL \xf7 COUNT. Like splitting a restaurant bill equally \u2014 each person pays the 'mean' amount. Sum everything, divide by how many. That's it."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the median? How do you find it for odd and even number of values?",back:"The median is the middle value of a data set arranged in ascending (or descending) order. For ODD number of values (n): Median = ((n+1)/2)th term. Example: 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 (n=5) \u2192 Median = 3rd term = 7. For EVEN number of values (n): Median = average of (n/2)th and (n/2+1)th terms. Example: 3, 5, 7, 9 (n=4) \u2192 Median = (5+7)/2 = 6.",memory_hook:"Median = MIDDLE. Arrange data first \u2014 always! Odd values: pick the exact middle. Even values: average the two middle ones. Like the median strip on a highway \u2014 exactly in the centre."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the mode of a data set? Can there be more than one mode?",back:"The mode is the value that appears most frequently in a data set. Example: 3, 5, 3, 7, 3, 5, 8 \u2192 Mode = 3 (appears 3 times). A data set can have: No mode (if all values appear once: 2, 4, 6, 8). One mode \u2014 unimodal (most common). Two modes \u2014 bimodal (4, 6, 4, 7, 6 \u2192 modes are 4 and 6). Multiple modes \u2014 multimodal. Note: mode is the only measure of central tendency that can be used for non-numerical data too (e.g., 'most popular colour').",memory_hook:"Mode = MOST. The value that MOST often appears. Fashion MODE is what's most popular \u2014 same idea. If everyone wears blue, blue is the 'mode' colour."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the range of a data set? Find the range of: 15, 42, 7, 28, 53, 19.",back:"Range = Maximum value \u2212 Minimum value. Range measures how spread out the data is \u2014 a large range means the data is spread widely; a small range means it is clustered. Example: Data = 15, 42, 7, 28, 53, 19. Maximum = 53. Minimum = 7. Range = 53 \u2212 7 = 46. Range doesn't tell you about the shape of distribution, only the total spread.",memory_hook:"Range = MAX minus MIN. Like the 'range' of a mountain \u2014 how far from the lowest to highest point. Large range = widely spread. Small range = tightly clustered. MAX \u2212 MIN = RANGE."},{id:"fc05",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"When should you use mean vs median to represent data? Give an example.",back:"Mean is best when data has no extreme outliers \u2014 it uses all values and is most mathematically precise. Median is better when there are outliers (extreme values) that would distort the mean. Example: 5 workers earn: \u20b910,000, \u20b912,000, \u20b911,000, \u20b913,000, \u20b91,00,00,000. Mean = \u20b910,09,200 (misleading \u2014 distorted by the millionaire). Median = \u20b912,000 (accurate representation of the typical worker's income). Government uses median income because a few billionaires distort the mean.",memory_hook:"One billionaire in a room makes EVERYONE a millionaire on AVERAGE (mean). But the MEDIAN doesn't change \u2014 it stays at the middle person's income. Mean = fairest math, Median = fairest for skewed data."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you construct a pie chart? What is the formula for sector angle?",back:"To construct a pie chart: (1) Find the total of all values. (2) Calculate sector angle for each category: Angle = (Category value / Total) \xd7 360\xb0. (3) Draw a circle and mark each sector using a protractor. (4) Label each sector. Verify: sum of all sector angles = 360\xb0. Example: Favourite fruits (total=60): Mango=24, Apple=18, Banana=12, Others=6. Mango angle = (24/60)\xd7360\xb0 = 144\xb0. Apple = 108\xb0. Banana = 72\xb0. Others = 36\xb0. Sum = 360\xb0 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Pie chart = 360\xb0 pizza. Each slice angle = (your piece / whole pizza) \xd7 360\xb0. Bigger piece = bigger angle. Always verify: slice angles ADD to 360\xb0."},{id:"fc07",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"The mean of 6 numbers is 14. Five of them are 10, 16, 18, 12, 15. Find the sixth number.",back:"Mean = Sum/n \u2192 Sum = Mean \xd7 n = 14 \xd7 6 = 84. Sum of known five numbers = 10+16+18+12+15 = 71. Sixth number = 84 \u2212 71 = 13. Check: (10+16+18+12+15+13)/6 = 84/6 = 14 \u2713. This 'reverse mean' technique is very commonly tested.",memory_hook:"If mean is given: TOTAL = Mean \xd7 n. Then: missing value = Total \u2212 Sum of known values. Three-step shortcut: (1) Find total. (2) Find known sum. (3) Subtract."},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is probability? What is the formula for P(event)?",back:"Probability is a number between 0 and 1 that measures how likely an event is to occur. P(E) = Number of favourable outcomes / Total number of possible outcomes. Key facts: P(impossible event) = 0. P(certain/sure event) = 1. 0 \u2264 P(E) \u2264 1 always. P(E) + P(not E) = 1. Example: Rolling a die. P(getting 5) = 1/6. P(getting odd) = 3/6 = 1/2 (odd numbers: 1,3,5). P(getting 7) = 0 (impossible). P(getting \u22646) = 1 (certain).",memory_hook:"P = FAVOURABLE / TOTAL. Always between 0 and 1. P=0: no chance. P=1: definite. P=\xbd: 50-50. 'FATE': Favourable \xf7 All Total Events."},{id:"fc09",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A coin is tossed. Find: P(Head), P(Tail), P(Head or Tail).",back:"Sample space = {Head, Tail}. Total outcomes = 2. P(Head) = 1/2 (1 favourable out of 2 total). P(Tail) = 1/2. P(Head or Tail) = 2/2 = 1 (certain \u2014 tossing a coin MUST give either head or tail). P(Head) + P(Tail) = 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 \u2713. This confirms P(E) + P(not E) = 1.",memory_hook:"Coin: 2 outcomes (H, T). Each has probability \xbd. P(H)+P(T) = 1. The coin MUST land \u2014 so getting 'something' has P=1. This is the simplest probability experiment."},{id:"fc10",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A die is rolled once. Find P(getting an even number).",back:"Sample space when rolling a die = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}. Total outcomes = 6. Favourable outcomes (even numbers) = {2, 4, 6} = 3 outcomes. P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2. Note: exactly half the numbers on a die are even (1,3,5 are odd; 2,4,6 are even) \u2014 so P(even) = P(odd) = 1/2.",memory_hook:"Die probability shortcut: P(even) = 3/6 = \xbd. P(odd) = 3/6 = \xbd. P(prime) = {2,3,5}/6 = 3/6 = \xbd. P(getting \u22654) = {4,5,6}/6 = 3/6 = \xbd. Many die events have probability \xbd \u2014 coincidence or not, it simplifies many problems!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A bag has 4 red, 6 blue, and 2 yellow balls. One ball is drawn at random. Find P(blue) and P(not blue).",back:"Total balls = 4+6+2 = 12. P(blue) = 6/12 = 1/2. P(not blue) = 1 \u2212 P(blue) = 1 \u2212 1/2 = 1/2. OR: P(not blue) = (red+yellow)/total = (4+2)/12 = 6/12 = 1/2. Verify: P(blue)+P(not blue) = 1/2+1/2 = 1 \u2713.",memory_hook:"P(not E) = 1 \u2212 P(E). This is the COMPLEMENT rule. Always faster to use when P(E) is easy to calculate. P(not blue) = 1 \u2212 P(blue). Complement = flip the probability (1 minus)."},{id:"fc12",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a double bar graph? When is it useful?",back:"A double bar graph is a bar graph with TWO bars for each category \u2014 one for each data set being compared. It is useful when you want to compare two related data sets across the same categories. Example: Comparing rainfall in Bengaluru and Mangaluru for each month of the year \u2014 two bars per month, different colours for each city. The bars are placed side by side with a small gap between them. A legend (key) identifies which bar is which.",memory_hook:"Double bar = TWO data sets, SIDE BY SIDE. Like comparing two players' batting scores across 5 matches \u2014 one red bar per match for Player A, one blue for Player B. Easy visual comparison."},{id:"fc13",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Find the mean, median, and mode of: 5, 8, 5, 7, 9, 5, 8, 10, 5",back:"Mean: Sum = 5+8+5+7+9+5+8+10+5 = 62. n=9. Mean = 62/9 \u2248 6.9. Median: Arrange: 5,5,5,5,7,8,8,9,10. n=9 (odd). Median = 5th term = 7. Mode: 5 appears 4 times (most). Mode = 5. Range = 10\u22125 = 5. Note: Mean (6.9) \u2260 Median (7) \u2260 Mode (5) \u2014 showing these are three distinct measures.",memory_hook:"Three different measures, three different answers. Mean=sum/count. Median=middle after sorting. Mode=most frequent. They CAN be equal sometimes, but usually give different perspectives on the SAME data."},{id:"fc14",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"In a pie chart, the sector for 'Science' has an angle of 72\xb0. What fraction of students chose Science? If 300 students were surveyed, how many chose Science?",back:"Fraction of students = sector angle / 360\xb0 = 72\xb0/360\xb0 = 1/5. Number of students = 1/5 \xd7 300 = 60 students. This is the reverse pie chart problem \u2014 given the angle, find the count. Formula: Count = (angle/360\xb0) \xd7 total. Verify: 60/300 = 1/5 \u2192 angle = 1/5 \xd7 360\xb0 = 72\xb0 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Pie chart reverse: angle/360\xb0 = fraction. Fraction \xd7 total = count. It's the pie chart formula flipped. 72\xb0/360\xb0 = 1/5 \u2192 1/5 of 300 = 60. Always: angle \u2192 fraction \u2192 count."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"The probability that it will rain tomorrow is 0.35. What is the probability that it will NOT rain?",back:"P(not rain) = 1 \u2212 P(rain) = 1 \u2212 0.35 = 0.65. The complement rule: P(event) + P(not event) = 1. Since the only possibilities are 'rain' and 'not rain', their probabilities must sum to 1. This is used in weather forecasting \u2014 if the forecast says '35% chance of rain', there is a '65% chance of no rain'.",memory_hook:"P(not E) = 1 \u2212 P(E). Always. The whole sample space has probability 1. If one event takes 0.35, everything else takes 1\u22120.35 = 0.65. Simple subtraction from 1."},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"The ages of 7 students are: 12, 13, 11, 14, 12, 13, 12. Find the mode and median.",back:"Mode: 12 appears 3 times (most frequent). Mode = 12. Median: Arrange: 11, 12, 12, 12, 13, 13, 14. n=7 (odd). Median = 4th term = 12. Here, mode = median = 12. This is a coincidence \u2014 they are not always equal. Range = 14\u221211 = 3.",memory_hook:"Arrange FIRST before finding median. Sort: 11,12,12,12,13,13,14 \u2192 middle is 4th = 12. Mode = 12 (appears thrice). When mode=median, the data is quite symmetric around 12."},{id:"fc17",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"List all possible outcomes when two coins are tossed simultaneously.",back:"When two coins are tossed simultaneously, the sample space is: {HH, HT, TH, TT}. Total outcomes = 4. Probabilities: P(two heads) = P(HH) = 1/4. P(exactly one head) = P(HT or TH) = 2/4 = 1/2. P(at least one head) = P(HH, HT, TH) = 3/4. P(no head) = P(TT) = 1/4. P(two tails) = 1/4.",memory_hook:"Two coins \u2192 4 outcomes: HH, HT, TH, TT. (2 coins = 2\xb2 = 4 total outcomes). List them systematically \u2014 first coin possibilities (H,T) \xd7 second coin possibilities (H,T) = 2\xd72 = 4. This systematic listing prevents missing outcomes."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"In a survey of 120 students, 45 prefer maths, 30 prefer science, 25 prefer English, 20 prefer Hindi. Find the sector angle for Maths in a pie chart.",back:"Total = 45+30+25+20 = 120. Maths sector angle = (45/120) \xd7 360\xb0 = (3/8) \xd7 360\xb0 = 135\xb0. Check all angles: Science = (30/120)\xd7360\xb0 = 90\xb0. English = (25/120)\xd7360\xb0 = 75\xb0. Hindi = (20/120)\xd7360\xb0 = 60\xb0. Sum = 135+90+75+60 = 360\xb0 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Pie chart construction: (value \xf7 total) \xd7 360\xb0 for each category. Always verify: all angles sum to 360\xb0. If they don't, there's a calculation error somewhere."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Cards numbered 1 to 20 are placed in a box. One card is drawn. Find P(prime number).",back:"Total outcomes = 20. Prime numbers from 1 to 20: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19 = 8 primes. (1 is NOT prime). P(prime) = 8/20 = 2/5. Key: always list the favourable outcomes explicitly to avoid miscounting. Students commonly include 1 or forget 2 \u2014 be careful.",memory_hook:"Primes 1\u201320: 2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19 = 8 primes. (1 is NOT prime \u2014 definition: prime has exactly 2 factors; 1 has only one factor). 2 is the ONLY even prime. Memorise primes up to 20 for probability questions."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Class test scores: 72, 85, 68, 91, 75, 82, 68, 79. Find mean, median, mode, and range.",back:"Mean: Sum = 72+85+68+91+75+82+68+79 = 620. n=8. Mean = 620/8 = 77.5. Median: Arranged: 68,68,72,75,79,82,85,91. n=8 (even). Middle values = 4th and 5th = 75 and 79. Median = (75+79)/2 = 77. Mode: 68 appears twice (most). Mode = 68. Range: 91\u221268 = 23. Summary: Mean=77.5, Median=77, Mode=68, Range=23.",memory_hook:"Full data handling workout: Sort first (for median and mode), then calculate. Mean needs all values. Median needs sorted order. Mode needs frequency count. Range = last \u2212 first (after sorting). Always sort FIRST."}]}},2956,[]);